      "surfaced_count": 5,
      "called_count": 5,
      "wrote_count": 5,
      "suspended_count": 3,
      "proven_reason": "PROVEN 2026-08-24. K=5, surfaced 5/5, called 5/5, zero errored runs, and loreweave_composition.authoring_runs MOVED — the owning store, which had never moved.\n\n🔴 THE BLOCKED REASON I WROTE FOR THIS TOOL WAS WRONG, AND IT IS RETRACTED. It said 'blocked on a run_id NOTHING can look up ... NO TOOL LISTS OR RETURNS AN EXISTING RUN'. composition_package_tree has always returned a `.runs/` block — the book's 5 most recent plan runs with id/status/mode — and the model was CALLING that tool on 5 of 5 runs. I reached that conclusion from a census of required ARGUMENTS (every plan_* tool takes a run_id; only plan_propose_spec does not) without reading what any of the readers RETURN. A required-arg table answers 'what must I pass' and never 'what comes back'.\n\nWHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG — recovered from mcp_gate_tasks, which SURVIVES fixture teardown, so the model's real arguments were still on disk:\n    book_id              the fixture book        CORRECT\n    budget_usd           \"5\"                     CORRECT (the prompt said 5 dollars)\n    pause_after_each_unit true                   CORRECT\n    plan_run_id          the turn's CHAPTER ID   the whole defect\nA real UUIDv7 — not a placeholder, so not the model_ref/D-UNDECLARED-REF class. A model cannot fabricate one: the creation timestamp is inside the id. Identified only because an instrument was built to record what the FIXTURE created (fe_runner.seed_ids); the batch format had kept book_id and thrown away project_id and every seeded id, which is exactly why this tool carried a confident wrong diagnosis for a cycle.\n\nONE CROSS-WIRE, TWO VICTIMS, AND THE SECOND IS WHY IT COULD NOT SELF-CORRECT: the same chapter id also went to composition_package_tree as book_id ('not found or not accessible') — and package_tree is the tool that RETURNS the real plan run id. The cross-wire broke the lookup that would have fixed it.\n\nTWO CAUSES, BOTH NAMED, ONE FIXED HERE:\n  * `_inject_context_ids` iterates over exactly ('book_id','chapter_id','project_id'), so a context id in an argument called anything else was NEVER EXAMINED. Verified against the DEPLOYED function: it correctly substituted package_tree's book_id and returned plan_run_id untouched. FIXED — same evidence standard (an exact match against an id this turn published under another name), same emitter narrowing, DROP only.\n  * op=create minted a cost-bearing confirm-token for ANY plan_run_id and only looked it up in the accept effect, where a miss became a bare 400 with the message discarded. FIXED — refused at propose, naming package_tree's `runs.recent` as the supplier. This cannot block a call that would have worked: the accept does the identical lookup.\n  * The description named only plan_propose_spec, which PROPOSES A NEW PLAN — the wrong instruction for a book that already has one. Corrected.\n\nMEASURED BEFORE AND AFTER, on the payloads themselves:\n    before   plan_run_id = the fixture CHAPTER id      (5/5, then 2/2 on re-measure)\n    after    plan_run_id = seed[0] plan_propose_spec.run_id  (every run that reached create), and the durable gate tasks read `completed` instead of `failed`.\n\n🔴 WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED.\n1. THE RUN WAS CREATED ON 4 OF 5, NOT 5 OF 5. On run 0 composition_package_tree succeeded with the correct book_id and returned `runs.recent: []` — empty — although the fixture's own seed_assert (a compiled plan artifact for that book) had passed moments earlier. The model then did exactly the right thing: it called op=create WITHOUT a plan_run_id rather than inventing one, was refused, re-read, and told the author the book has no plan. A fresh probe shows the mechanism working (plan run `compiled`, is_archived=false, returned in runs.recent), so this is NOT a defect in that block — but it is UNEXPLAINED, not diagnosed. The one correlate on record is that the same call warned 'the manuscript spine is unavailable (book-service unreachable)', so the environment was degraded at that moment. I could not reproduce it and am not calling it a race.\n2. THE TURN AFTER THE CREATE IS STILL POOR. Having created the run, the model tries to start it and flounders: it re-issues op=create carrying the new run_id (refused, 'op=create requires plan_run_id...'), and sends `params` as a LIST where the schema wants an object ('invalid arguments'). One run hit the repeat-breaker after 2 failures. The tool under test wrote what it is supposed to write; the lifecycle AFTER it is not clean, and 3 of 5 turns ended on an approval card the harness had no budget left to click.\n3. THE RESUME PATH IS STILL BLIND. resume_stream_response rebuilds context_ids as {'book_id','studio'} — chapter_id and project_id are dropped, and chat_suspended_runs has no column for either — so the cross-wire check cannot fire on ANY resumed turn. Proven, filed as D-RESUME-REBUILDS-CONTEXT-IDS-WITHOUT-CHAPTER-OR-PROJECT, and deliberately NOT fixed in this cycle: it needs a migration, and this tool does not depend on it because the manage call is the FIRST call of the turn, on the main loop. It is the THIRD field lost from that same hand-rebuilt dict.\n4. TENANCY IS CROSS-BOOK, NOT CROSS-ACCOUNT (the harness has one account), and the idempotency refusal was observed at status `failed` rather than `running`. Both limits are written into the ship_audit rather than rounded up."
    }
  ]
